Date: Tue, 14 Sep 93 14:16:12 MDT
From: Mark_Muhlestein@Novell.COM (Mark Muhlestein)
Subject: NANO: Ignorance on pages of The Economist

Was anyone else disappointed with the feature section in the Sept 11
"Economist" called "The Next 150 Years/The future surveyed"?

The section comprised a score of essays from "eminent writers,
thinkers, and politicians" predicting what the world would be like in
150 years.  The writers were encouraged to "think ahead as far as they
dared, but also to consider topics or themes in today's world that have
not been given enough attention and which could play an important role
in shaping the future."

I didn't carefully read every essay, but I was shocked that most of the
writers (Richard Dawkins excepted) appeared to assume few major effects
from new technology development.  In particular, as far as I saw, there
was only *one* reference to nanotech.  This was made by a former deputy
editor of "The Economist" in an essay titled "Some moral dilemmas,
1993-2143."  He begins his essay:

"During the next 150 years mankind will gradually or suddenly become
able to put matter together atom by atom, and bodies together virtually
gene by gene.  This means that nearly all diseases (including cancer
and AIDS) could be made rather easily curable, although this ought not
to conquer most of the process of ageing -- or rather, with luck, not
until after we are enlightened enough to stop it doing any such
terrible thing."

He goes on to forcefully argue that people should be allowed to live
for their "natural term," which he speculates might be about 95 years.
He predicts people will be encouraged to go gracefully, via euthanasia,
to avoid "a shortage of active young people, but a dreadful surplus of
ignorant old people."

Why is it that there is such a lack of imagination among these "eminent
writers, thinkers, and politicians?"  After all, the contents page of
"The Economist" declares its purpose as being "to take part in 'a severe
contest between intelligence, which presses forward, and an unworthy,
timid ignorance obstructing our progress.'"

This would seem to be a good opportunity for one or more of us to write
a response to "The Economist" to try to spread some Extropian memes.

Mark_Muhlestein@novell.com

