There are private alternatives to the post office -- UPS and Fedex,
for example. I pay most of my bills using Checkfree because I don't
like the post office. 

On the other side, if you are using a service that someone is charging
for, there is an implicit contract there. You don't sign something
when you go into McDonalds and order a Burger, but its still theft if
you don't pay when they hand you the burger.

> Should people encourage government services and growth?

No, but its another thing to encourage stealing. I can see an argument
that you shouldn't pay $.29 for postage -- but I see little argument
that mail should be free, period. Even if the postal service were
private mail would not be free, so its unreasonable to think that
"free" is a good price for mail service. Again, this isn't like being
forced to pay income tax -- you are buying a service, and there is no
claim on your part that you are not asking for a service and that they
are not delivering one to you.

