I could be a brain in a vat. I acknowledged so in my post. 

The injections I am getting from the simulation are real sense data.
If I'm a brain in a vat hooked to a simulator, then the simulator is
*real*, and so my sensations caused by it are of reality. Perhaps I
will never figure out that I am a brain in a vat from the data I
receive, but that sense data is both real and all I've got that might
allow me to figure it out.

As far as Rand goes, I believe she might deny she might be a brain in
a vat, but that denial would come because she rejects that arbitrary
assertions are possible. I don't, if for no other reason than to
communicate with others who have different standards, but I have a
fair amount of sympathy for her position.

> For all you really know, you have never seen what the real world looks
> like. The laws of physics you've grown up with, the vistas you've
> seen, everything could just be the imagination of a demented computer
> program.

Sure. Never denied it.

In fact, I don't think Objectivists would deny it, except for their
rejection of arbitrary assertions.

I was surprised to find one day that Objectivists are not naive
realists. The 3-d physical universe is not a primary, but a derivative
concept from sense data.

> You say "unequivocally" that your senses "have to do with reality". I
> would suggest that you are completely unsafe in so doing.
> 
> It *IS* safe to say "I have hypothesized that my senses are yielding a
> reasonably accurate model of an objective real world, and I have yet
> to have seen falsifying evidence for this hypothesis, and I see lots
> of positive evidence for it, so I continue to believe it." 

Perhaps again it might help to say that I do not consider sense data
to "model" anything. It is data. Input. You might make a mistake in
what you do with it, but it is not right or wrong.

> However, to
> hold ANYTHING "unequivocally" is the real denial of reality. Reality
> is that you don't actually know the answer and can't, and might as
> well admit it, at least to yourself.

"The" answer? 

Perhaps it would make you happier if I spoke in modelspeak. I am quite
comfortable doing so, and in fact believe that the subjective
modellers are not doing it enough. 

I consider Rand's metaphysical axioms a good methodological basis for
epistemology. Holding them "unequivocally" is a statement that I will
use that method consistently. Indeed, that method is about
consistency as I described in my previous post.

